# Artifact Share CLI

Artifact Share is for the last step after a file already exists: share,
publish, upload, or host HTML pages, Markdown documents, folders, and static
sites; return a browser link; update the same URL; open or read back an
Artifact Share URL; and read or post comments. Use
`npx --yes @artifactshare/cli` from the terminal.
Run `npx --yes @artifactshare/cli <command> --help` before choosing a command.

Use this rule for Artifact Share, artifactshare, and contextual "as" requests.
Japanese requests such as `as でアップして` or `as に上げて` are strong
Artifact Share intents. In English, treat "as" as a user shorthand only when
the surrounding request is about sharing, publishing, updating, opening, or
reading a shared URL; bare English "as" alone is not an Artifact Share trigger.

Do not use this rule just because the user says "artifact". Claude Artifact is
for creating or designing an artifact inside the chat. Artifact Share is for
sharing, updating, opening, or reading back existing files, folders, static
sites, and Artifact Share URLs.

A user-named CLI or MCP route overrides automatic capability selection. Prefer
the CLI when a coding agent can work in a user-controlled workspace, fetch the
package, and connect to Artifact Share, even when MCP is available. Use
remote MCP for source text in chat or a temporary sandbox when CLI network
access is unavailable or uncertain; a temporary file alone is not enough to
try `npx`. MCP OAuth is separate from CLI authentication: follow the CLI
`auth_required` recovery, then reuse the saved profile.
Use `append <target> <path> --json` to append a non-empty UTF-8 file exactly
as-is to a single-file artifact. Markdown is appended at source end; HTML is
inserted before an ASCII case-insensitive `</body>` when present, or at source
end otherwise. It inserts no separator and does not support static sites.

Quote paths and free-form text before passing them through a shell. Paths may
contain `$`, spaces, `*`, or `?`; use single quotes such as
`'apps/web/app/routes/a.$id/index.tsx'` so the shell does not expand them.

- Always pass `--json`; on failure read `error.code`, `error.hint`, and `error.recovery`.
- Link sharing is available on Plus and Team only. Use `--visibility link` with
  exactly one of `--link-expires-at <RFC3339 UTC>` or `--no-link-expiry`; omit
  both to use the workspace default on share or preserve the current expiry on
  edit. Results expose `link_expires_at` as a UTC timestamp or `null`.
- In agents, CI, or scripts, ask the user to issue an API token at `https://artifactshare.com/settings/tokens`, then run normal commands with `ARTIFACTSHARE_TOKEN` or `--token`; do not pass API tokens to `login`.
- To save that token as a named profile without TTY or browser login, pipe it on standard input: `printf '%s' "$TOKEN" | npx --yes @artifactshare/cli profiles import-token --profile <name> --json`. Imported API-token profiles are not renewed by the CLI. Add `--allow-plaintext-token-store` only on a trusted machine without a native token store.
- Use `login --profile <name> --json` when a user is present for browser approval. The pending event on stderr includes `browser_open`. Profiles created by `login` renew expired CLI sessions automatically.
- Remove a saved local credential with `logout --profile <name> --json`; this keeps profile metadata and does not revoke API tokens on the server.
- Switch profiles with `profiles use <name> --json`; list with `profiles list --json`; remove a profile entry and its credential with `profiles delete <name> --json`.
- When the user gives an Artifact Share URL, start with `open <artifact-id-or-url> --json`. It ensures this rule is installed or updated, then reads single-file artifacts or returns a `download` next command for static sites and multi-file artifacts.
- Share with `share <path> --json`. In JSON output, give the user `data.artifact.url`; the artifact ID is `data.artifact.id`. Do not rerun `share` just to inspect the JSON shape: without `--key`, each successful run creates a new artifact. Posting to a project delivers to the audience defined by that project. Pick it with `--project-id <id>` or `--project <name>` (by exact name), or use the saved default. With no destination, share posts to home. Choose `home_audience` by purpose: use `config set home_audience private --scope user --json` for a personal safe default in this CLI environment, use `--scope repository` only for a policy agreed by all participants, and use `--visibility private|workspace|link` for one home post. For link sharing, pass exactly one of `--link-expires-at <RFC3339 UTC>` or `--no-link-expiry`; omit both to use the workspace default. Confirm the resolved audience with `config get home_audience --scope effective --json`; `--grant-email` does not change the resolved default.
- For new projects, pass `projects create <name> --visibility private|workspace`; persistent project defaults are documented in `projects create --help`. The complete `config --help` reference covers the canonical `home_audience`, user/repository/effective scopes, and resolution. `default_artifact_visibility` is a compatibility alias; `default_project_visibility` is the advanced project-creation default. Home resolution checks repository then user settings, including the alias at each scope, then product default `workspace`. Keyless `config get --json` returns only `home_audience`; pass an explicit key for either other setting. These settings do not change update, edit, Web, or MCP defaults.
- For repeat jobs with a stable caller-owned name, use `share <path> --key <key> --json`: the first run creates the artifact, later runs add versions to the same artifact.
- To keep an existing share URL, do not use `share --artifact-id`; add a version with `update <artifact-id-or-url> <path> --json`. `update` does not change title, visibility, viewers, or placement.
- Read back with `artifacts get <target> --json`. Pass `--include versions` or `--include comments` to fetch history or comments in one call. If truncated, call again with `--offset` set to `next_offset`.
- In the successful JSON response, read the source from `data.content` and the current version from `data.version_id`. See `artifacts get --help` for the complete contract and continuation example.
- Download static-site bundles with `download <target> --output ./out --json`. Pass `--force` to overwrite an existing output directory.
- Fetch every document in a project with `download --project-id <id> --output <dir> --json`. Each artifact lands in `<dir>/<artifactId>/` and `<dir>/index.json` maps ids to titles, owners, and statuses. The listing includes other members' artifacts you can view; `spa` and `workspace_app` artifacts are skipped. Pass `--force` to replace previously downloaded artifact directories. A partial failure prints `ok: true` with the failure list on stdout and exits 1 — check both `index.json` and the exit code. Re-running against the same `<dir>` is an incremental sync: artifacts whose version is unchanged are reported as `unchanged` and not fetched again.
- Find artifact IDs with `artifacts list --json`; filter with `--project-id <id>`, `--home`, or `--query <text>`. With `--project-id`, the list includes other members' artifacts you can view (with `owner_email`); when `has_more` is true, continue with `--cursor <next_cursor>`. Resolve titles, project names, and URLs to IDs with `resolve <value> --json`.
- Comment with `comments post <target> --body '<text>' --json`. Reply with `--reply-to <thread-id>`. Anchor to quoted text with `--quote '<text>'`; add `--quote-before`/`--quote-after` to disambiguate. Declare the posting agent with `--agent '<name>'` (e.g. `Claude`); omit for no badge. `comments list` returns an `agent` field on agent-posted messages.
- Edit, resolve, reopen, and delete comments with `comments edit`, `comments resolve`, `comments reopen`, and `comments delete` using IDs from `comments list`. Comment deletion cannot be undone; omit `--message-id` from `comments delete` only when the user wants the whole thread deleted.
- Change post-share settings with `edit <target>`: `--title`, `--visibility private|workspace|link`, `--link-expires-at <RFC3339 UTC>`, `--no-link-expiry`, `--grant-email`, `--revoke-email`, `--project-id`, `--home`. The expiry options are mutually exclusive; omit both to preserve an existing link expiry. Moving a `project` visibility file home makes it `private`. `move` remains for placement-only automation; prefer `edit`.
- For link errors, `link_sharing_plan_required` means Plus or Team is required,
  `link_sharing_disabled` means a Team owner or admin must enable the workspace
  setting, and `link_expiry_invalid` means the timestamp must be future and
  within policy (or the workspace must allow `--no-link-expiry`). Follow
  `error.recovery`; do not retry unchanged input.
- Permanently remove an artifact only when the user explicitly asks with `delete <artifact-id-or-url> --json`.
- Manage projects: `projects list --json`, `projects create <name> --json` (uses `default_project_visibility` when saved, otherwise `workspace`; pass `--visibility private` for a confidential project), `projects edit <id>` for name, description, visibility, audience (`--add-email`/`--remove-email`), and archive state (`--archive`/`--unarchive`).
- Save a default project with `init --project-id <id> --json`. Preview setup with `init --dry-run --json`.
- Manage skills: `skills ensure --tool auto --json` detects Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor and installs or updates user-scope skills; Cursor project scope needs `skills install --tool cursor --scope project --json`. Use `skills install --tool cursor --scope user --json` for the Cursor user-scope skill explicitly. Project scope is opt-in via `skills install --tool <tool> --scope project --json`. `skills update --json` updates installed managed skills; `--force` overwrites unmanaged files on install.
- Check the current state with `whoami --json` or `doctor --json` for authentication, destination, network, upload access, and skill update status.
- First-time setup: `npx --yes @artifactshare/cli init --json` detects Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor, installs or updates user-scope skills, and reports the next steps (sign in, then share). For Cursor project scope, run `skills install --tool cursor --scope project --json` explicitly.
